            "ev:7fe7fbcc37cbed9843bcf1f6a0b885e42beaa4e2d119c31a19285f80ae48a482"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7406058/"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Problems, failure modes and limitations",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "The APTA clinical practice guideline for physical therapist management of Parkinson disease states that physical therapists should recommend community-based exercise to reduce motor disease severity and improve nonmotor symptoms, functional outcomes, and quality of life, rating evidence strength as high and recommendation strength as strong. (high confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9046970/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:8ad4f70b53ddce5cfe5857495a57ec09d7d9623cdc8f4892c8ab641a83b34e0a",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:9a93cbff512de6ace7c0a7fc28effa0aaee8c70734482473c4841aa9af0b6c04",
            "ev:a027243bc8c8fba40bf5d759b391815ae1826a2a091e71979fddd0abb2b67f6a"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11687919/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In the Li et al. randomized trial (NCT00611481), 195 patients with Hoehn and Yahr stage 1 to 4 Parkinson's disease were assigned to tai chi, resistance training or stretching for 60-minute sessions twice weekly for 24 weeks, and the tai chi group performed better than the resistance-training and stretching groups on the primary outcome of maximum excursion (between-group differences of 5.55 and 11.98 percentage points) and on directional control (10.45 and 11.38 percentage points). (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285459/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:58f3cb3adc87d9c67abbaf23561d5871f3f876436e45338529105e87059ca882",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:1929db20b36a219c200b1dfbd8c5fa891234d63c901e7b633a3d1e031597f291",
            "ev:8b251e4dc8d2cb68083c394f03c0699ca9a60df494c6af589a84bd27a254ad8a",
            "ev:952abfce32b486909e1fc7fbbc964ecccd539517aec4492b36acf4842984e1f0",
            "ev:a08072b9efcbed77804f195079419284c5c8b8b72f05487900c82f30cd88dd89",
            "ev:ad081eb5059dd6158cf26acfdbd03e172e3247ad9c24f0ce70652d2c6ab67c51"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5618798/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Meta-analytic findings on tai chi and qigong and quality of life in Parkinson's disease differ: Song et al. (15 RCTs) reported improved quality of life (ES=-0.393, p<.001), whereas a meta-analysis of seven systematic reviews (Fidan et al.) and a meta-analysis of thirteen RCTs of traditional Chinese exercises (Song et al., later version) reported no statistically significant quality-of-life effect. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5618798/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:e807f9086c59e7172797416637066d47672a9447d0dff8a2578124db58444862",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:4ed63285b771fd5d72097bbb99de1ff4abc1265b1dca033c841b3173d581a7df",
            "ev:8e0a8408318d8addff925e4169803d94c17a3d61add3a6d985817e0bb5fcccbb",
            "ev:b40ee7f5bc33a134e9b35d18bd70b997b3c3e6db2c5f06e937b3b82328fd9b83",
            "ev:b6c5cad9c9f6f1b02ab2cc62d22be5386a17d2369b7cb79dd1e339099cc4dea6",
            "ev:b9b325fd13ae7ed931760e734284a202f7d1a34d09d5aa891c28b095fc6b4193",
            "ev:f6312cc6aebb7717936d662c2d6f9ff5393dfc2a9b67d65215de46f0166edce5"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31116118/",
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12847013/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Review authors state quality limits on the dance literature: an umbrella review of 55 systematic reviews rated the overall confidence in the results of 38 reviews (69%) as critically low; a systematic review of 20 RCTs (N = 723) reported that small samples and methodological limitations resulted in low-certainty evidence across outcomes; and a systematic review of sixteen RCTs noted that performance bias cannot be controlled because participants and personnel cannot be blinded to a dance intervention and that less than half of the studies used a low-risk randomization procedure. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11687919/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:797720b9b7d03e10b522285c1392c4bbe1d382ac89a9b29f68fdee970f046d85",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:05af76198c3980bd084c435b97e88f0546b6b29f846164e8dffac1167447484e",
            "ev:094a0135d2dc4ed93d066d7a38c7f5ce963649fe4369a2399a42d386b697d6e2",
            "ev:0d6a923c295c7c417a9237f600b8ff5a65a42b7df3f3ac248d7ea43a2c2ac988",
            "ev:a9e7f3084a0b06f0f3c0bc8d4a1cb015a2b08cebf4003eb60281957f75227588",
            "ev:c7d345ced4f8407713835bc08e39c4839a115b8f2ef6fa67dfe919b5fbf991c8",
            "ev:f21520be97501711168bd2d5330f68c3212a04a6561ebcb94dc2cb211605d012"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8456607/",
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7406058/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Systematic reviews of boxing for Parkinson's disease differ: Wang et al. (13 studies, 402 patients, pre-post assessments of boxing interventions) conclude that boxing exercise can effectively improve both motor and non-motor symptoms with safety and high adherence, whereas Gonzalez-Devesa et al. (13 studies) report no significant pooled effect on functional mobility, balance, motor symptoms, gait and cardiorespiratory fitness and call the evidence uncertain, and Hernandez-Martinez et al. (eight studies, 100 older people) conclude that boxing does not significantly improve balance, cardiorespiratory fitness and health-related quality of life. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11983467/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:d2bc4ab8b1dc27f3cc0d643ed6fbd8d0c2637faf2aa653b5112c7736dfcc44c2",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:1b145c604f1542a37ccef536fd76b1cf8e1505187a31888b7a6e26f7e9bc316d",
            "ev:1d9acd8ad8512a2796f0a646e27b3d1815ddb46860721c7ca6fad3a0b6fcc1c0",
