            "ev:35513021a8c499e4c07122910a72b9b212ac394cce82b7a7274e6ab60458b7bb",
            "ev:5273adb6754abe641ef34d778ba092f30a75dedced70a1489eac45ffaa123153",
            "ev:6b5639605f02a337f8383cc627e192e75aedca3b61d3694df8f46b1486e4b920",
            "ev:8f086cd18663344e6c79598238eb261a0f3a4560bc3a7d395f8cbff4833a27a0"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11778260/",
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12179875/",
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6904341/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Meta-analyses of yoga in Parkinson's disease report benefit with caveats about robustness: Ban et al. (ten studies, 359 participants) report better motor status (MD = −5.64) with yoga and state that their results should be interpreted with caution, Suárez-Iglesias et al. report that a meta-analysis on five RCTs found yoga more effective than passive control for motor symptoms, whereas a network meta-analysis of mind-body exercises (Bai et al.) found that the significant effects of yoga did not prove robust in sensitivity analysis. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8275425/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:db69cff9e514c938de186f02091d51f83053f172d82499695fe2b336041e912f",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:009d60bc2c8484cb0fc66899d6024b5016da7e81f7bbc3008e19ac67ad0eac41",
            "ev:6c0f9ec6da80a6ce4cf74e3dafe43c456ee1550828552bc277c9ff52125b0232",
            "ev:9d799092c632050322776a7258acbb5f78736a92a8545285d54b5a055d19dd94",
            "ev:d49a2e464db3fdda710bd5e32752aef0ac2a66839f767c8839a0dc8a7fb8f3ab",
            "ev:e8fd49307c22002db6bc9fabceb26a9f4b9746445e42bdd202aaccb29d5cba34",
            "ev:f97825f6a52e445739eb4f887afcd1014262e90dd97d058847fdafc36f1a58d2"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34533097/",
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13751929/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Systematic reviews of Nordic walking in Parkinson's disease differ in how they characterize benefit: Salse-Batán et al. (twelve studies) report that none of the statistically significant benefits were clinically meaningful except for walking ability and that Nordic Walking does not lead to clinically significant changes in global motor impairment, functional mobility, balance and physical fitness, whereas De Santis and Kaplan (13 studies, 318 patients) report that symptom severity, walking speed and gait pattern consistently improved after 4 to 24 weeks of Nordic walking relative to baseline or any control condition during ON-phases of pharmacotherapy, while rating study quality as poor to moderate. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35593147/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:60f89dfab9d40a2a30a8307cb66e2783314a71d4a571a11c89505f8f42d7cd05",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:271082c049dca5ac230708c33277786ced3037d0f59966b4ed08cdd55658b7e8",
            "ev:6c5126f5119bdc3e7053daa88405c2942d509fcc323ec248e3830fd146fbb04c",
