    {
      "title": "Facts and statements",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "On dance and motor symptom severity the sources differ: the European Physiotherapy Guideline (first edition, December 2014) gives a weak recommendation against using dance to improve movement functions (UPDRS III), whereas two meta-analyses of randomized trials (Ismail et al., 20 RCTs; Carapellotti et al., sixteen RCTs) report better MDS-UPDRS scores with dance than in non-dancers or no-intervention controls. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
          "source": "https://www.parkinsonnet.nl/app/uploads/sites/3/2019/11/eu_guideline_parkinson_guideline_for_pt_s1.pdf",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:8695b89bc356bc79b08e2953a77a1290b999b0e365fc2ad4f5b6bab8ef432fbd",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:094a0135d2dc4ed93d066d7a38c7f5ce963649fe4369a2399a42d386b697d6e2",
            "ev:0a5a11527c5e832feb6e494702c0d6647c06cb1ac9c82f42ae2cd976c10c09cf",
            "ev:2fd47a87d47c89ef450ece2c4c78e58ffc5964a1932ffd7ff77e4f0d8f404c17",
            "ev:3b597d6d81da25952366b06cc0b2cdbb0fe4d5c5930c4dc3579d7aa1a2718607",
            "ev:a9e7f3084a0b06f0f3c0bc8d4a1cb015a2b08cebf4003eb60281957f75227588",
            "ev:bda0f409a64c79f5db4c449ff65c2afd0217ff04509cbdf97a78150a71b05e50"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8456607/",
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7406058/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The European Physiotherapy Guideline (first edition, December 2014) gives a strong recommendation for using tai chi to improve movement functions (UPDRS III) and weak recommendations for using it to improve number of falls, balance capacity (BBS), walking speed, stride length, walking distance, functional mobility (TUG), muscle strength and functional reach, based on three CCTs in which 200 pwp participated. (low confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
          "source": "https://www.parkinsonnet.nl/app/uploads/sites/3/2019/11/eu_guideline_parkinson_guideline_for_pt_s1.pdf",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:13c71ada27d204dbce2dfe2c2f01b4c1759073d9747f084d12d319983240bdd7",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:3b597d6d81da25952366b06cc0b2cdbb0fe4d5c5930c4dc3579d7aa1a2718607",
            "ev:aab53b72634272c58c8e93d36e121a06049112e64f68e093d74ac651ecbfd730",
            "ev:e7dda446bfc8e1478d4313858c4e18cdaa9e374de100363773f31f298743bd04"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In a systematic review of sixteen RCTs involving 636 participants with mild to moderate PD, Carapellotti et al. reported that dance was favored over no intervention on MDS-UPDRS III (-2.31 points, p = 0.0004; four studies, n = 160) and balance (0.50 points, p = 0.0007; five studies, n = 206) but not on forward gait velocity (p = 0.44) or PDQ-39 (p = 0.65); an umbrella review of 55 systematic reviews (Pinto et al.) reported that dance added to usual care was better than usual care alone for motor symptoms severity, depressive symptoms, balance and functional mobility but not for gait distance and quality of life. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7406058/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:851d6e9ac12e0a03b00a4ad29bbed92afd6a745c41a76d9df81c05c0acdf7f2c",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:094a0135d2dc4ed93d066d7a38c7f5ce963649fe4369a2399a42d386b697d6e2",
            "ev:0a5a11527c5e832feb6e494702c0d6647c06cb1ac9c82f42ae2cd976c10c09cf",
            "ev:0d6a923c295c7c417a9237f600b8ff5a65a42b7df3f3ac248d7ea43a2c2ac988",
            "ev:1288255e73f5d8ff4c5c88424ffb9b5c0625bcb8b232fcd3ba0ad739c710af26",
            "ev:504ad5d8752e054d11403b09bed59da60b6481b4ed376ab9f4a282488a24c1d7",
            "ev:5abe76b12c925f879ec619612efff64fa106019f737d3b369c0a22ee5a18e0af",
            "ev:8643c7e7371024993d840fd9d89ca8b797a2c5799519f164d3f1b6cbf318e6a9",
            "ev:b67d0cbe52ce77141bfad948e8dd3bb0d2f810b623596cb263704c5ea7fbe71a",
            "ev:cd721f1c49688bd6d166b26e587f01b6ae340f6830a1ff9a606a2528fc77a7f0",
            "ev:ed22450507317f92236b273e0eb344356cbc79c556f3c894dc0c0356d42aded8",
            "ev:f08fe392f386e42a02b05ad591e40b5845426b6476e23ccabe9763b7b50004ea"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11687919/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Two randomized trials compared boxing with another active program: in Combs et al. (n = 31; 24-36 sessions of 90 minutes over 12 weeks) the traditional exercise group had significantly greater gains in balance confidence than the boxing group while only the boxing group showed significant improvements in gait velocity and endurance, and in Sangarapillai et al. (40 participants, 20-week trial) the sensory exercise group improved in comparison to the boxing group on a group-by-time interaction for the UPDRS-III (P = .036). (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23422464/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:0c2edb06361a4d22bce1e36b8b88349e4db7227c083194933275c69152447de6",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:435feae30a93eeeb281542191f2c39a93b053e6b942e7b12b06a461ba71f7d47",
            "ev:92ddf79cdd20d3fb9464ee85b4a703bc7fe3ed3e571cc68110065b78de097888",
            "ev:98bf0000d435faa50a40ce1e01300db4e849ededa2afbb328e952bf3bcd97752",
            "ev:9cda2a8c47333755e535682abc744f168dd178852f1c72a4e436f0a8dc31a043",
            "ev:cdd604cce04bcd1d54f3bbee56fbd1837057b6b61bce9489dc84356ea1451a75",
            "ev:e9501684da9b150dde061cff9c6291b088a679433d290f2ecc6f09faa5a00b87"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414806/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Adverse-event findings reported in the sources read: the APTA guideline reports that studies found no significant differences in adverse events between community-based exercise and the comparison groups, Li et al. observed no serious adverse events in their tai chi trial, and Ban et al. state that none of the included articles in their yoga meta-analysis reported serious adverse events. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9046970/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:0ac8c54b12b45b1c134baa9ee98162cf06b3f150bb7e7665b2e82772759b673a",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
